005 | — | HLR-3 (P1) is regression prevention, not a stated prerequisite for the P0 submission; kept parallel so P0 certification work is not serialized behind P1 work. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n|-----|----------|-------|\n| HLR-1 (images run on FIPS-enabled clusters with validated crypto) | P0 | E002, E003 (scope input from E001) |\n| HLR-2 (compliance evidence package assembled and submitted) | P0 | E001 (module inventory), E005 (assembly and submission) |\n| HLR-3 (CI enforces FIPS regressions with gating check) | P1 | E004 |\n| HLR-4 (documentation of FIPS support posture and prerequisites) | P2 | E006 |\n\nAll HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Key Decomposition Decisions\n\n- **No Investigation epics.** The strategy's Open Questions section states \"None\n  on scope\". The crypto module inventory (\"several images\" link non-validated\n  crypto) is not a genuine unknown that changes which epics exist — the\n  remediation, CI, and evidence epics exist and proceed the same way regardless\n  of which specific images are flagged (failure = fix-and-retry). The inventory\n  is also a mandatory deliverable of HLR-2, so it is modeled as an\n  Implementation epic producing an artifact (Rule 13 serial edges), not an\n  Investigation.\n- **Platform Operator treated as an existing component.** It is a canonical\n  RHAI Jira component (present in `.context/rhai-components.txt`) and the\n  strategy references its existing images being remediated, not provisioned.\n  However, it has no architecture-context file (only `kserve.md` exists for this\n  stack), so its FIPS posture cannot be validated against architecture context —\n  see Health Warnings. No repo-onboarding chain was generated because the\n  strategy does not introduce a new component.\n- **Component/team assignments.** KServe is owned by RHOAI Serving per\n  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`. The strategy\n  contains no Impacted Teams table, so the Platform Operator epic is assigned to\n  RHOAI Platform (assumption, flagged below). The evidence-package epic and the\n  docs epic are anchored to the serving stack owner (KServe / RHOAI Serving) as\n  the coordinating team; the docs epic uses the canonical `Documentation`\n  component.\n- **docs-authoring priority exception.** E006 maps to P2 HLR-4 but is exempt\n  from priority inheritance; its priority is derived from the strategy\n  frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0.\n- **External gates.** Laboratory certification (six-to-nine month externally\n  controlled turnaround) and security review board sign-off at defined\n  milestones are modeled inside E005 as acceptance criteria and process gates,\n  not as DAG edges to epics the strategy does not control.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external certification laboratory is a third party with uncertain,\nexternally controlled timing, and partial value is deliverable without it:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent value):** E001 (inventory), E002/E003 (remediated\n  images that run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters), E004 (CI regression\n  enforcement). Together these deliver HLR-1 and HLR-3 without certification.\n- **Tier 2 (externally gated):** E005 — evidence package submission through the\n  security review board to the lab and certification outcome (completion of\n  HLR-2). Lab scheduling and review-board cadence cannot be compressed by\n  engineering effort.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections:** no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies\n  table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements,\n  no Technical Approach. Acceptance criteria below were derived from the HLRs and\n  problem statement; team assignments for Platform Operator are inferred.\n- **Platform Operator lacks architecture context.** No architecture-context file\n  exists for it, so claims about its role in the serving stack could not be\n  validated against architecture context (reflected in its AI signals). If the\n  operator were in fact a new/not-in-platform component, an onboarding chain\n  (repo-onboarding + license-validation → konflux-onboarding) would need to be\n  prepended.\n- **Schedule risk, not priority inversion:** the P0 certification path (E005) is\n  wall-clock-dominated by external human/third-party gates regardless of\n  engineering speed; the strategy itself flags this. CI enforcement (HLR-3) is\n  only P1 — reasonable, since it prevents regressions after remediation rather\n  than enabling the certification submission.\n- **Scope trap noted:** \"no non-validated crypto in the request path\" (HLR-1) is\n  narrower than \"no non-validated crypto anywhere in the image\". The\n  remediation epics scope to the request path per HLR-1; the inventory (E001)\n  records the full module picture so the evidence package (E005) can speak to\n  the distinction.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":30264,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-819e9677085ef2b9","agent_id":"a23d34c4666fd53a0","session_id":"95f10158-af0c-4809-b17b-36c6f2e3eed3","uuid":"ae33177f-ba1e-4808-98de-76ee00690e8b","timestamp":"2026-10-21T18:37:35.471Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9013 strategy"}
