{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9df6673e0b14ddbc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: E001 is anchored to component KServe / team RHOAI Serving while its\n10\t    inventory scope includes the Platform Operator image; acceptable as a single logical\n11\t    audit artifact but the cross-component attribution could be stated more explicitly\n12\t    in the epic frontmatter\n13\t- severity: minor\n14\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n15\t  description: E005 component field reads KServe for a stack-wide compliance evidence\n16\t    package (coordinating-team anchor); a stack-level component would be more precise\n17\t- severity: minor\n18\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n19\t  description: E002/E003 lack an explicit rollback AC; image rebuilds have inherent\n20\t    rollback via prior image tags, but stating it would make the AC set more explicit\n21\terror: null\n22\t---\n23\t## Review Summary\n24\t\n25\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n26\tRecommendation: accept\n27\t\n28\t## Criterion Details\n29\t\n30\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n31\tAll four HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 (P0) → E002/E003 with E001 scope input; HLR-2 (P0) → E001 (inventory deliverable) + E005 (assembly/submission); HLR-3 (P1) → E004; HLR-4 (P2) → E006. The traceability matrix is present and complete. Priority inheritance is correct: E001, which blocks all P0 work, is P0; E004 (P1, HLR-3) blocks no downstream implementation work except the docs epic, which is exempt. No priority collapse — the P1 (CI enforcement) and P2 (documentation) HLRs were split into separate epics E004 and E006 so they can be deferred independently, exactly as the planning requirement demands. E006 is docs-authoring and its P0 priority correctly derives from the strategy's `priority: Critical`, not from inheritance.\n32\t\n33\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n34\tCross-checked every diagram edge against epic frontmatter `dependencies` in both directions: all 11 edges match exactly, arrow convention is dependency → dependent as required. No circular dependencies, no references to nonexistent epics, no branch files. All edges are justified under the rules: the four E001 fan-out edges are genuine Rule 13 data/artifact dependencies (the inventory defines remediation targets, the CI check's pass/fail classification, and is itself a required evidence-package component — not a mere image digest or URL reference); E002/E003 → E005 carry build attestations and test results; E001–E005 → E006 are Rule 11 docs-authoring edges (valid, not flagged). Parallelism is preserved: E002 ∥ E003 (different repos, no shared artifacts) and E004 ∥ E002/E003/E005 — the decomposition explicitly keeps the P1 CI work from serializing the P0 certification path, with reasoning recorded. No konflux-onboarding or repo-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable (and no onboarding chain is warranted — no new component is introduced; Platform Operator is confirmed present in `.context/rhai-components.txt`). Critical path is 4 (E001→E002→E005→E006), within the M-standard 3–4 heuristic. Scanned epic content for uncaptured data dependencies — none found; every consumed artifact (inventory, attestations, classification, implementation state) has a corresponding edge.\n35\t\n36\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n37\tE002 (KServe + data plane, RHOAI Serving) and E003 (Platform Operator, RHOAI Platform) are correctly split along the component/team boundary. No scope duplication between siblings — each epic builds distinct artifacts. Code implementation and content authoring are separated (E006 is a dedicated docs-authoring epic). No epic exceeds ~2 weeks of engineering effort (E005's six-to-nine-month span is external lab wait time, not work). Two minor observations, both documented decisions in the summary rather than defects: E001 is anchored to KServe/RHOAI Serving although its inventory scope includes the Platform Operator image (acceptable as a single logical audit artifact — splitting it by component would fragment the deliverable), and E005's component field reads \"KServe\" for what is a stack-wide evidence package (coordinating-team anchor). The RHOAI Platform assignment for E003 is an inference (no Impacted Teams table in the strategy) but is explicitly flagged in Health Warnings and is the reasonable reading.\n38\t\n39\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n40\tNo Investigation epics, and the decision not to have any is explicitly justified and sound: the strategy's Open Questions section is empty on scope, the module inventory is itself a mandated HLR-2 deliverable (so Implementation is the right type), and the downstream epic set does not change based on which images the inventory flags (failure mode is fix-and-retry). Every epic produces a concrete artifact: inventory (E001), rebuilt images with attestations (E002/E003), a gating CI check (E004), a submitted evidence package (E005), published documentation (E006). No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set, consistent with the absence of Investigations. External human/third-party gates (review board, lab) are correctly modeled as acceptance criteria and process gates inside E005 rather than DAG edges to epics the strategy does not control.\n41\t\n42\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n43\tAll six epics are Implementation type and all carry `ai_signals` (none carry `investigation_signals`) — correct rubric. Every frontmatter signal value matches its rationale-file entry, and the rationales are consistent with the strategy content and with the architecture context, which I verified directly: only `kserve.md` exists in `.context/architecture-context/`, so E003's `architecture_claims: -1` (Platform Operator claim unverifiable against architecture context) is justified; `kserve.md` confirms RHOAI Serving ownership and Konflux onboarding, corroborating E004's `pattern_precedent: 1` and the team assignments. E005's negative signals (`pattern_precedent`, `existing_foundation`, `external_dependency`, `human_process_gates` all -1) match the strategy's statements that no evidence package exists today and that lab/review-board gates dominate the schedule. E002's `change_specificity: 0` (specifics determined by inventory outcome) is honest. No signal contradicts rubric conditions.\n44\t\n45\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n46\tEvery epic has concrete, testable ACs derived from the HLRs and problem statement (e.g., \"models deploy and serve inference requests successfully\" on a FIPS-enabled cluster; \"a build that reintroduces a non-validated crypto module fails the gating check\"). The docs-authoring epic carries the rule-mandated technical-review AC (\"technically reviewed against the implementation … before publication\"). No konflux-onboarding chain exists, so the build-pipeline-green AC is not applicable; E004's own gating-enforcement AC covers the pipeline concern. E005's ACs sensibly distinguish submission/triage (engineering-completable) from lab turnaround (schedule constraint). No epic body references siblings by draft ID. One minor: E002/E003 replace crypto dependencies but state no explicit rollback AC; since these are image rebuilds where the prior image tag remains deployable (inherent rollback) rather than component/system replacements, this is an explicitness nit, not a rule violation.\n47\t\n48\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n49\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: both affected components have remediation epics, all four HLRs are covered, and the out-of-scope list (workbenches/pipelines/training, Common Criteria) is respected. Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: the externally controlled lab and review-board cadence appears in E005's ACs and the summary's tiered delivery; the request-path versus whole-image scope trap from HLR-1 is explicitly handled (E002/E003 scope to the request path, E001 records the full module picture so E005 can speak to the distinction). Cross-epic completeness holds: E001's inventory of all serving-stack images is collectively consumed by E002 (KServe + data plane), E003 (operator), E004 (scans all serving-stack images), and E005 (evidence for every image in scope) — nothing silently dropped. HLR-4 requires documentation and the docs-authoring epic E006 exists, covering posture, prerequisites, verification guidance, and CI-failure interpretation.\n50\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-862531359d50711e","agent_id":"afa810437c52592cd","session_id":"95f10158-af0c-4809-b17b-36c6f2e3eed3","uuid":"4b727e5c-aa84-4bab-86d8-be063bf26558","timestamp":"2026-10-21T18:44:30.867Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9df6673e0b14ddbc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
