he evidence\npackage; Rule 11 edges into docs-authoring are valid and not flagged as\nserialization. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding\nepics exist, so no mandated-parallel pair is violated. Parallelism is\npreserved: E002 ∥ E003 (different repos) and E004 ∥ remediation are explicitly\njustified. Critical path E001→E002→E005→E006 = 4, matching the declared\n`critical_path_length: 4` and the M-strategy heuristic (3-4). No uncaptured\ndata/artifact dependencies found in epic content — the classification (E001→\nE004) and attestations (E002/E003→E005) consumptions are all edged.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (1/2)\nNo epic bundles multiple teams' distinct deliverables, no scope duplication\nbetween siblings (E002/E003 remediate disjoint image sets; E001 produces the\nclassification E004 encodes), no work-product type mixing (E006 is pure\ndocs-authoring), and no epic exceeds sprint-scale engineering effort (E005's\n6-9 month wall clock is external lab waiting, not work). However, three minor\nboundary issues accumulate:\n- E001 attributes the full-stack inventory — including Platform Operator\n  images — to KServe/RHOAI Serving. Defensible as one logical audit artifact\n  (the same-logical-change exception, explicitly documented), but the\n  operator-image audit work falls in RHOAI Platform's territory without their\n  ownership; the rubric's own example of a boundary edge case (\"shared\n  utility code attributed to one team when two teams contribute\") matches\n  this shape.\n- E004's CI check scans Platform Operator builds and wires a gating step into\n  the operator's build pipelines, yet is attributed entirely to KServe/RHOAI\n  Serving — same pattern, second instance.\n- E005's component `Model Serving Stack` is not in the canonical\n  `.context/rhai-components.txt`; `Serving Runtimes` is the closest canonical\n  parent-level name. The mapping is logged in the summary, and the\n  component-name rule's own example uses a non-list parent-lev